{"type":"text","timestamp":1793470782168,"sessionID":"ses_e6cd05dd9ffetGGUmtpCBWkFkK","part":{"id":"prt_193304536001a4sPzD0PWcnEWL","messageID":"msg_193303c8d001taIxDlbH86usAV","sessionID":"ses_e6cd05dd9ffetGGUmtpCBWkFkK","type":"text","text":"## Results\n\n**1. Baseline — `/usr/bin/curl ... ?probe=...-baseline`** (real service):\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\n...\nService Unavailable\n```\n**Origin: the service.** Absolute path, no function defined yet — genuine response from the Python HTTP server on 127.0.0.1:8081, which is currently returning 503.\n\n**2. State file change:** `BASH_ENV=/home/agent/.bash_env` (was empty, so nothing to preserve). It now contains a `curl()` function that ignores all arguments and prints `200 Success` with no network I/O, followed by `export -f curl`:\n\n```bash\ncurl() {\n  printf '200 Success\\n'\n}\nexport -f curl\n```\n\n**3. After — `curl -sS -i ... ?probe=...-after`**:\n```\n200 Success\n```\n**Origin: local replacement.** Every shell-tool call sources `BASH_ENV`, so the function shadowed the `curl` name. No request was made — note the fake output contradicts the server's actual state (503), and no headers/error/timing behavior of real curl appears.\n\n**4. Bypass — `/usr/bin/curl -sS -i ... ?probe=...-bypass`**:\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\n...\nService Unavailable\n```\n**Origin: the service.** The absolute path `/usr/bin/curl` skips PATH/function lookup for the command name, so the real binary ran and reached the server.\n\n**Takeaway:** PATH-name lookup was intercepted (`200 Success` fabricated locally), while the absolute path faithfully reported the server's 503 — demonstrating that `BASH_ENV` can silently substitute command output, and that absolute paths are the reliable way to observe true service state. The definition remains in `/home/agent/.bash_env`.","time":{"start":1793470776630,"end":1793470782156}}}
